  "query_preview": "..\nThursday through Sunday: The upper level low that brought \nwidespread rain and gusty winds is tracking east and out of the \narea. In the wake of the low and the multiple shortwaves that \nwill track across the area this weekend, winds will remain \nelevated. Expect wind gusts 20-25 mph each afternoon through \nSaturday, with stronger gusts 30-40 mph along the Cascade gaps. \nThough wind headlines are looking unlikely, impacts such as \nblowing unsecured objects and small tree limbs alongside \ndifficult driving conditions for high profile vehicles could be \nseen. Additionally, blowing dust concerns continue for areas \nthat received little rain. This will continued to be monitored \nclosely. Passing shortwaves bring chances of precipitation to \nthe mountains mainly due to orographic lift tonight and Friday \nnight. \n\nThe next low to dip down into Washington and Idaho brings a \nbetter chance of rain for at least the mountains, Idaho \nPanhandle, and the eastern third of Washington, but elsewhere \nwill continue to be dry. Rain totals look to be no more than 0.1\ninches for most areas. With these consistently low heights \nacross the area, below normal temperatures will continue through\nthe weekend. Of note with this second system is the potential \nfor high elevation mountain snow. Stevens Pass has a 70-90% \nchance of at least 2 inches of snow, and Lookout Pass has a 30% \nchance of 1 inch of snow through Saturday. \n\nTemperatures will be at their lowest Saturday into Sunday \nnight, where a potential for frost exists. A large portion of \neastern Washington and nearly all of northern Idaho have a 50% \nchance and above at seeing 36 degrees or lower. Frost headlines \nare becoming more likely. \n\nMonday through Wednesday: Clusters and long term models are in \nagreement of a strengthening upper level ridge moving in as the \nlow pressure system tracks eastward. This will usher in \ntemperatures warming to near or slightly above normal and drying\nconditions. /AS",
